<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 1600 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Shalom Aleichem</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">שָׁלוֹם עֲלֵיכֶם</bdi> / <bdi lang="he-Latn">Shalom Aleichem</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Hebrew song sung at home on Friday evening to welcome the angels that are said to come with you when the Sabbath begins.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous (the kabbalists of Safed)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (many tunes; the best known by Israel Goldfarb, 1918)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Jewish communities</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Safed, present-day Israel</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>late 16th or early 17th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by the kabbalists of Safed (late 16th or early 17th century))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>Shalom Aleichem, &quot;Peace be upon you&quot;, is a song that many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> sing every Friday night on coming home from the synagogue. It marks the arrival of the Shabbat by welcoming the angels who are said to accompany a person home on the Sabbath eve, and among religious Jews it is commonly sung before Eshet Ḥayil and the Kiddush. Many sing each stanza three times.</p><p>The liturgical poem was written by the kabbalists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safed">Safed</a> in the late 16th or early 17th century; Chaim Leiberman compiled a survey of all its surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and its earliest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> is in Tikunei Shabbos. It is thought to rest on a teaching of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a>: two angels, one good and one evil, walk home with a person from the synagogue on Friday night. If the house is ready for the Shabbat, with the lamp lit and the table set, the good angel blesses it that the next Shabbat be the same and the evil angel must answer &quot;Amen&quot;; if it is not ready, the evil angel wishes the same and the good angel must answer &quot;Amen&quot;.</p><p>The words have been argued over. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilna</a> (1720–1789) warned singers not to pause in the middle of one phrase, since a pause changed its sense. Rabbi Jacob <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emden">Emden</a>, in his prayer book Bet El (1745), objected to the hymn altogether, holding that petitions on the Sabbath and petitions to angels were improper, and he also faulted its grammar and struck out a prefix; a few later prayer books followed him, among them Seligman Baer's Siddur Avodat Yisroel (1868), although the change makes the metre awkward. Jacob Zallel Lauterbach (1873–1942) judged some of the words not original, and Moshe Yair Weinstock and others criticised the last verse for seeming to hurry the angels on their way. Mizrahi and some Sephardi traditions add a verse before the last; Yaakov Chaim Sofer, in his Kaf Hachayim, explained the Sephardi custom of repeating the request for a blessing between the added verses, so that the angels are asked to bless their coming, their rest and their leaving, which answers the complaint that the singer seems to send them away.</p><p>There are many tunes. The slow one most people know was written by the American rabbi, composer and conductor Israel Goldfarb on 10 May 1918, as he sat by the Alma Mater statue in front of Low Memorial Library at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a>, and published that year in Friday Evening Melodies by Israel and his brother Samuel. It is often taken for a traditional Hasidic melody; in 1963 Goldfarb wrote that many people believed it had come down from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-sinai">Mount Sinai</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>. A traditional Moroccan Jewish melody is the same as the song El Rey Nimrod. Later versions include a joyful one popularised by Idan Yaniv and Kinderlach in September 2009, a faster tune by Rabbi Shmuel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, and the version Debbie Friedman shared with Rabbi Joy Levitt as one of her last acts, which she believed would be her legacy.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A greeting of peace to the angels who, in Jewish teaching, come into the home as the Sabbath begins. The singer welcomes them, asks them to bless the household with peace, and bids them farewell. Its mood is warm and festive, a domestic welcome of the holy day.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Hebrew</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Hebrew</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">שָׁלוֹם עֲלֵיכֶם מַלְאֲכֵי הַשָּׁרֵת מַלְאֲכֵי עֶלְיוֹן</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">shalom 'aleikhem mal'akhei hasharet mal'akhei 'elyon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Peace be upon you, ministering angels, angels of the Most High,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">מִמֶּֽלֶךְ מַלְכֵי הַמְּלָכִים הַקָּדוֹשׁ בָּרוּךְ הוּא</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">mimmelekh malkhei hammelakhim hakkadosh barukh hu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from the King of the kings of kings, the Holy One, blessed be he.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בּוֹאֲכֶם לְשָׁלוֹם מַלְאֲכֵי הַשָּׁלוֹם מַלְאֲכֵי עֶלְיוֹן</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">bo'akhem leshalom mal'akhei hashalom mal'akhei 'elyon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Your coming be in peace, angels of peace, angels of the Most High,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">מִמֶּֽלֶךְ מַלְכֵי הַמְּלָכִים הַקָּדוֹשׁ בָּרוּךְ הוּא</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">mimmelekh malkhei hammelakhim hakkadosh barukh hu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from the King of the kings of kings, the Holy One, blessed be he.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בָּרְכֽוּנִי לְשָלוֹם מַלְאֲכֵי הַשָּׁלוֹם מַלְאֲכֵי עֶלְיוֹן</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">barkhuni leshalom mal'akhei hashalom mal'akhei 'elyon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bless me with peace, angels of peace, angels of the Most High,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">מִמֶּֽלֶךְ מַלְכֵי הַמְּלָכִים הַקָּדוֹשׁ בָּרוּךְ הוּא</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">mimmelekh malkhei hammelakhim hakkadosh barukh hu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from the King of the kings of kings, the Holy One, blessed be he.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">צֵאתְכֶם לְשָלוֹם מַלְאֲכֵי הַשָּׁלוֹם מַלְאֲכֵי עֶלְיוֹן</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">tsetkhem leshalom mal'akhei hashalom mal'akhei 'elyon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Your going be in peace, angels of peace, angels of the Most High,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">מִמֶּֽלֶךְ מַלְכֵי הַמְּלָכִים הַקָּדוֹשׁ בָּרוּךְ הוּא</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">mimmelekh malkhei hammelakhim hakkadosh barukh hu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from the King of the kings of kings, the Holy One, blessed be he.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional liturgy. Text from <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%A9%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%9D_%D7%A2%D7%9C%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%9D_%D7%9E%D7%9C%D7%90%D7%9B%D7%99_%D7%94%D7%A9%D7%A8%D7%AA" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (SBL general-purpose).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>The refrain line is repeated in every stanza of the original and is translated again where it stands.</li><li>lines 2.1 and 4.1: 'bo'akhem leshalom' and 'tsetkhem leshalom' are verbal nouns, literally 'your coming (be) in peace' and 'your going out (be) in peace'.</li><li>line 1.2: 'mimmelekh' ('from the King') ties the angels to God as their sender; 'King of the kings of kings' is literal (God above the Persian 'king of kings').</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="4TJLwnx_B3s" data-title="Shalom Aleichem: Cantor Marcus Feldman, Israel Goldfarb's melody"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Cantor Marcus Feldman, Israel Goldfarb's melody</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Cantor Marcus Feldman. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4TJLwnx_B3s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sholom Aleichem, in Friday Evening Melodies (Israel and Samuel Goldfarb)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1918</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new melody, now the best known</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Shalom Aleichem (Idan Yaniv and Kinderlach)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2009</span><span class="sg-chain-h">popular new version of the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Shalom Aleichem (Debbie Friedman)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Shalom Aleichem (Shmuel Brazil)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new, faster melody</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Talmudic teaching of the two Sabbath angels</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the hymn is assumed to be based on it</span></li></ul></section>
